#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9edad51-52ff-5e4d-a083-87a8011e0eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{5}{3}]∪[\\frac{2}{3}, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082101} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5bb541-b61c-5801-b76b-a63a881a7c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 1]∪[3, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082102} {:answer/id #uuid "10c98059-6990-5b95-af14-11bd8ffa16c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-1]∪[3, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082103} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb897cb-07e8-5cdd-8eb4-23b010420846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{4}{3}]∪[- \\frac{2}{3}, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082104} {:answer/id #uuid "5f07f913-b32e-58e0-96fd-ce1f50332c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞, \\frac{1}{3}]∪[\\frac{8}{3}, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb897cb-07e8-5cdd-8eb4-23b010420846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{4}{3}]∪[- \\frac{2}{3}, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082104} :answer-field/id #uuid "044c9a88-ebb9-5349-aa07-969b4322e39a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aefbcfa3-c2fe-5122-b5fc-5fd4c61eed65" :question/math-academy-id "q-66061" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94056ffb-a25a-5ff5-bd39-f8615c37e2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277492} {:answer/id #uuid "b8eaf9c3-5baa-50a8-81be-e05759fa97a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{5}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277493} {:answer/id #uuid "93784874-7b2f-5cbc-b194-9fadc3e5e3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277494} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1aaa8-637f-504f-aece-60323fd2869b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277495} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7fbd0a-e4ca-5b8d-a277-dcb2c637ff82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1aaa8-637f-504f-aece-60323fd2869b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277495} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1674ac0-d8f5-5250-bd18-3da2707e75d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f78b0f00-abf4-5ba2-a6f5-e47b01f3518d" :question/math-academy-id "q-66062" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca53a8a-1b88-5d73-92f7-c3a7d3a55291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{5n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277499} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5c154f-9bde-5c41-94d3-fd235425290b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277500} {:answer/id #uuid "b39457fc-d1b9-5ea2-bb3d-948b759c3a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277501} {:answer/id #uuid "139cacce-77e7-5028-9844-38189882985f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277502} {:answer/id #uuid "29066276-cd13-57d1-ae56-f489abde8c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "139cacce-77e7-5028-9844-38189882985f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277502} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ee9aac-3118-5863-b25a-cee61da864d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24f4f8ab-4bd4-54bd-a8d1-e84a41a0a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66071" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4ba651-acd3-5302-b420-7cd5dac6e2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277506} {:answer/id #uuid "a1fc413f-4e85-5cdc-abb5-65381d8df7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277507} {:answer/id #uuid "80fcdfd2-fb65-5a15-bda2-1e049725d4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277508} {:answer/id #uuid "6cab8561-0644-5440-b8c1-2436e66c42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277509} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cb0a2b-5a99-5bd3-9ba9-28c4b1938fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80fcdfd2-fb65-5a15-bda2-1e049725d4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277508} :answer-field/id #uuid "f182d070-f5e6-573f-8fb5-47eca6ca7fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5da223d5-ac9d-59a3-bcda-b66c2be933a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66074" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d99fcb4-c4c0-5442-8e8b-b03a9bf7fd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277513} {:answer/id #uuid "e036d14f-0ff9-5080-a743-fff0bb7fc1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277514} {:answer/id #uuid "8841341b-0412-5c16-a615-9fd2bb055091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277515} {:answer/id #uuid "a1722eae-ef03-506a-b22b-4dcc68c9bd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277516} {:answer/id #uuid "058fbcdd-dd21-557c-8cfd-bee11b2fda89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8841341b-0412-5c16-a615-9fd2bb055091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277515} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e90bbce-22bc-5a9c-9520-bfbfb49f5446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04a71c1a-0ab2-549e-ba81-a58fecdbea24" :question/math-academy-id "q-66087" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points."}]}
